NNAAMMEE
     rroouuttee — manually manipulate the routing tables

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     rroouuttee [--ddnnqqttvv] [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [[_m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r _._._.] _a_r_g _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     At system start, routing tables are initialised and configured by
     netstart(8).  The rroouuttee utility can be used to view or manually manipu‐
     late the network routing tables.  Only the superuser may modify the rout‐
     ing tables.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd      Run in debug-only mode, i.e. don't actually modify the routing
             table.

     --nn      Bypass attempts to print host and network names symbolically when
             reporting actions.  The process of translating between symbolic
             names and numerical equivalents can be quite time consuming, and
             may require correct operation of the network; thus it may be ex‐
             pedient to forgo this, especially when attempting to repair net‐
             working operations.

     --qq      Suppress all output.

     --TT _r_t_a_b_l_e
             Select an alternate routing table to modify or query.  The de‐
             fault is to use the current routing table.  The current routing
             table can be displayed with id(1).

     --tt      Write routing messages to a fake device (_/_d_e_v_/_n_u_l_l) instead of a
             real routing socket to test route manipulation.

     --vv      Print additional details.

     The commands are as follows:

     rroouuttee [--ddnnqqttvv] [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] aadddd [_m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r] _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n _g_a_t_e_w_a_y
     rroouuttee [--ddnnqqttvv] [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] cchhaannggee [_m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r] _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n _g_a_t_e_w_a_y
           Add a new route, or modify an existing route, to the host or net‐
           work at _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n.  _g_a_t_e_w_a_y is the next-hop intermediary by which
           packets should be routed.  See _A_d_d_r_e_s_s _n_o_t_a_t_i_o_n _a_n_d _i_n_t_e_r_p_r_e_t_a_t_i_o_n
           for more information.

           A number of modifiers can be used with aadddd and cchhaannggee, and as docu‐
           mented with the other commands:

           _f_l_a_g_s   Various flags can be set on routes (viewable using sshhooww):

                   --bbllaacckkhhoollee  silently discard packets
                   --cclloonniinngg    generates a new route on use
                   --iiffaaccee      destination is directly reachable
                   --lllliinnffoo     validly translates address to link address
                   --mmppaatthh      multiple gateways for a destination exist
                   --nnoossttaattiicc   pretend route added by kernel or daemon
                   --pprroottoo11     sets protocol specific routing flag #1
                   --pprroottoo22     sets protocol specific routing flag #2
                   --rreejjeecctt     emits an ICMP unreachable when matched
                   --ssttaattiicc     manually added route (default)

                   The --bbllaacckkhhoollee and --rreejjeecctt flags require a _g_a_t_e_w_a_y to the
                   loopback interface, either 127.0.0.1 or ::1.

           [--lloocckk | --lloocckkrreesstt] --eexxppiirree _n
           [--lloocckk | --lloocckkrreesstt] --mmttuu _n
                   Specify the lifetime for the route (e.g. if generated by a
                   redirect) or the Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) size for
                   this path, respectively.  The value _n is locked if preceded
                   by --lloocckk; if preceded by --lloocckkrreesstt all following --eexxppiirree
                   and --mmttuu metrics are locked.

           --hhoosstt | --nneett
                   Interpret _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n as a host or network, respectively.

           --iiffaa _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
           --iiffpp _i_f_n_a_m_e
                   Where the destination and gateway are not sufficient to
                   specify the route, these modifiers may be used to determine
                   the interface address (--iiffaa) or name (--iiffpp).

           --llaabbeell _l_a_b_e_l
                   Associate the route with a _l_a_b_e_l.  Route labels can be used
                   to attach arbitrary information to a route.

           --mmppaatthh  Used to enter multiple gateways for the same destination
                   address (multipath).  When multiple routes exist for a des‐
                   tination, one route is selected based on the source address
                   of the packet.  The sysctl(8) variables
                   _n_e_t_._i_n_e_t_._i_p_._m_u_l_t_i_p_a_t_h and _n_e_t_._i_n_e_t_6_._i_p_6_._m_u_l_t_i_p_a_t_h are used
                   to control multipath routing.  If set to 1, multiple routes
                   with the same priority are used equally; if set to 0, the
                   first route selected will be used for subsequent packets to
                   that destination regardless of source.

           --mmppllssllaabbeell --iinn _l_a_b_e_l --ppuusshh|--ppoopp|--sswwaapp [--oouutt _l_a_b_e_l]
                   For MPLS routes, specify an ingress LSR to associate a par‐
                   ticular label to an IPv4/IPv6 route.  The MPLS traffic --iinn
                   and --oouutt modifiers are intended to identify the ingress la‐
                   bel and, optionally, the outgoing one.  Additionally, one
                   of the following operations must be used: --ppuusshh, --ppoopp or
                   --sswwaapp.  The route's gateway can be specified using the
                   --iinneett or --iinneett66 modifier before the address.

           --nneettmmaasskk _m_a_s_k
           --pprreeffiixxlleenn _l_e_n
                   Used to add subnet routes with the specified netmask.  The
                   netmask should be specified after the _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n parame‐
                   ter.  If no netmask is specified, an implicit one is used
                   for the AF_INET family.  The network mask can also be spec‐
                   ified as a prefix length, but in that case one of either
                   --iinneett or --iinneett66 must also be specified.

           --pprriioorriittyy _n
                   Specifies a routing priority.  If no priority is specified,
                   the kernel will set a priority depending on the RTF_STATIC
                   flag to either RTP_STATIC or RTP_DEFAULT.  Note that prior‐
                   ity 1 is reserved for kernel use.

     rroouuttee [--ddnnqqttvv] [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] ddeell[eettee] _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n [--pprriioorriittyy _n] [_g_a_t_e_w_a_y]
           Delete the route to _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n.  If multiple routes to the desti‐
           nation exist, a specific route must be selected by specifying the
           priority and/or a gateway.

     rroouuttee [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] eexxeecc _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g _._._.]
           Execute a command, forcing the process and its children to use the
           routing table and appropriate routing domain as specified with the
           --TT _r_t_a_b_l_e option.

     rroouuttee [--nnqqvv] [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] fflluusshh [_f_a_m_i_l_y] [--iiffaaccee _i_f_n_a_m_e] [--pprriioorriittyy _n]
           Delete all gateway entries from the routing table, optionally lim‐
           ited to a specific address family.  Routes matching a specific in‐
           terface or priority can be flushed by using the --iiffaaccee or --pprriioorriittyy
           modifiers.

     rroouuttee [--nnvv] [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] ggeett _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n [--pprriioorriittyy _n] [_g_a_t_e_w_a_y]
           Display the route to _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n.  If multiple routes to the desti‐
           nation exist, a specific route may be selected by specifying the
           priority and/or a gateway.

     rroouuttee [--nn] [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] mmoonniittoorr [_f_a_m_i_l_y] [--iiffaaccee]
           Continuously report any changes to the routing information base.
           The information reported can be limited to a specific address fam‐
           ily, a specific routing table using the --TT option, or interface
           specific messages (link state changes) using the --iiffaaccee modifier.

     rroouuttee [--ddttvv] [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] nnaammeesseerrvveerr _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s _._._.]
           Broadcast a list of up to five nameserver address proposals to
           resolvd(8), which is used to update the list of nameservers for the
           given interface in resolv.conf(5).  If no address is given, a re‐
           quest to remove the nameservers for the given interface is sent.

     rroouuttee [--nnvv] [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] sshhooww [_f_a_m_i_l_y] [--ggaatteewwaayy] [--llaabbeell _l_a_b_e_l]
           [--pprriioorriittyy _n]
           Display the routing table.

           If --ggaatteewwaayy is specified, only routes whose gateway are in the same
           address family as the destination are shown.

           If --llaabbeell is specified, only routes with the specified label are
           shown.

           If --pprriioorriittyy is specified, only routes with the specified priority
           are shown.  It may be specified by number or one of llooccaall,
           ccoonnnneecctteedd, ssttaattiicc, oossppff, rriipp, or bbggpp.  If the priority is negative,
           then routes that do not match the numeric priority are shown.

           Within the output of sshhooww, the "Flags" column indicates what flags
           are set on the route.  The mapping between letters and flags is:

           1    RTF_PROTO1       Protocol specific routing flag #1.
           2    RTF_PROTO2       Protocol specific routing flag #2.
           3    RTF_PROTO3       Protocol specific routing flag #3.
           B    RTF_BLACKHOLE    Just discard packets.
           b    RTF_BROADCAST    Correspond to a local broadcast address.
           C    RTF_CLONING      Generate new routes on use.
           c    RTF_CLONED       Cloned routes (generated from RTF_CLONING).
           D    RTF_DYNAMIC      Created dynamically (by redirect).
           G    RTF_GATEWAY      Dest requires forwarding by intermediary.
           H    RTF_HOST         Host entry (net otherwise).
           h    RTF_CACHED       Referenced by gateway route.
           L    RTF_LLINFO       Valid protocol to link address translation.
           l    RTF_LOCAL        Correspond to a local address.
           M    RTF_MODIFIED     Modified dynamically (by redirect).
           m    RTF_MULTICAST    Correspond to a multicast address.
           n    RTF_CONNECTED    Interface route.
           P    RTF_MPATH        Multipath route.
           R    RTF_REJECT       Host or net unreachable.
           S    RTF_STATIC       Manually added.
           T    RTF_MPLS         MPLS route.
           U    RTF_UP           Route usable.

     rroouuttee [--TT _r_t_a_b_l_e] ssoouurrcceeaaddddrr [--iiffpp _i_f_n_a_m_e | _a_d_d_r_e_s_s]
           Set the preferred source address to _a_d_d_r_e_s_s.  The source address
           can be set to the address assigned to interface _i_f_n_a_m_e if --iiffpp is
           specified, or reset by setting the address to zero.  If no argu‐
           ments are given, the preferred source addresses are printed.  The
           preferred source address is not used if the destination is on-link
           or the source address is assigned to a disabled interface.

   AAddddrreessss nnoottaattiioonn aanndd iinntteerrpprreettaattiioonn
     Addresses are assumed to be IPv4 unless they contain a colon, in which
     case they are treated as IPv6.  Alternatively they may be specified as
     belonging to a particular address family using one of the following modi‐
     fiers:

           --iinneett      IPv4 addresses; see ip(4)
           --iinneett66     IPv6 addresses; see ip6(4)
           --lliinnkk      hardware (link-level) addresses
           --mmppllss      MPLS addresses
           --ssaa        actual _s_o_c_k_a_d_d_r data, in hexadecimal format

     _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n is assumed to be a route to a network if any of the following
     apply:

     ••   the --nneett modifier is used
     ••   it is the word "default", equivalent to 0/0 or ::/0
     ••   it is an address with a “/_X_X” suffix, where _X_X is the number of bits
         in the network portion of the address
     ••   it specifies the network portion either with --nneettmmaasskk or --pprreeffiixxlleenn

     If _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n is a valid IP address or host name, or the --hhoosstt modifier
     is used, it is assumed to be a route to a host.

     All symbolic names specified for a _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n or _g_a_t_e_w_a_y are looked up
     using gethostbyname(3).

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/hosts   host and network name database
     /etc/mygate  default gateway address

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     For commands other than eexxeecc, the rroouuttee utility exits 0 on success, and
     >0 if an error occurs.

     For the eexxeecc command the rroouuttee utility exits with the exit status of
     _c_o_m_m_a_n_d if it could be invoked.  Otherwise the rroouuttee utility exits with
     one of the following values:

     1       An invalid command line option was passed to rroouuttee or setting the
             routing table failed.

     126     _c_o_m_m_a_n_d was found but could not be invoked.

     127     _c_o_m_m_a_n_d could not be found.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Show the current IPv4 routing tables, without attempting to print host‐
     names symbolically:

           $ route -n show -inet

     Add a static inet(4) route to the 192.168.5.0/24 network via the
     192.168.0.1 gateway:

           # route add -inet 192.168.5.0/24 192.168.0.1

     Amend the inet(4) route to the 192.168.5.0/24 network to use the
     192.168.0.2 gateway:

           # route change -inet 192.168.5.0/24 192.168.0.2

     Delete the inet(4) route to the 192.168.5.0/24 network:

           # route delete -inet 192.168.5.0/24

     Add a static inet6(4) route to a host which is on the vio0 interface that
     is outside your prefix, and use that host as a default gateway, as used
     by some hosting providers:

           # route add -inet6 2001:db8:efef::1 -cloning -link -iface vio0
           # route add -inet6 default 2001:db8:efef::1

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     %%ss:: ggaatteewwaayy %%ss ffllaaggss %%xx  The specified route is being added to or deleted
     from the tables.  If the gateway address used was not the primary address
     of the gateway (the first one returned by gethostbyname(3)), the gateway
     address is printed numerically as well as symbolically.

     %%ss %%ss ddoonnee  When the fflluusshh command is specified, each routing table entry
     deleted is indicated with a message of this form.

     NNeettwwoorrkk iiss uunnrreeaacchhaabbllee  An attempt to add a route failed because the
     gateway listed was not on a directly connected network.  The next-hop
     gateway must be given.

     nnoott iinn ttaabbllee  A ddeelleettee operation was attempted for an entry which wasn't
     present in the tables.

     rroouuttiinngg ttaabbllee oovveerrffllooww  An aadddd operation was attempted, but the system
     was low on resources and was unable to allocate memory to create the new
     entry.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
